@@ -9,13 +9,19 @@
 
 ## Live play
 
-- `Game` snapshots the title, prize count, and ordered, active questions when the moderator clicks **Start a game**. Each launch has a unique join code. Quiz edits apply to subsequent launches; deleting the quiz also deletes its games (with SQLite foreign-key cascades).
+- `Game` snapshots the title, prize count, round time limit, and ordered, active questions when the moderator clicks **Start a game**. Each launch has a unique join code. Quiz edits apply to subsequent launches; deleting the quiz also deletes its games (with SQLite foreign-key cascades).
 - A signed, game-scoped browser cookie is set before joining. That lets a reload or retry find the same player, even if the response to **Join** was lost. No account or cross-game identity is needed.
 - Game transitions, joins, and answer writes are transactional. Each moderator transition and answer carries the revision of the screen it's acting on. A delayed action cannot change a later question, and the unique player/round index ensures only one answer counts per player. The phone serializes rapid taps and sends the latest pending choice next.
 - Action Cable broadcasts a small game-scoped change notification after each committed phase or prize update. The message has no question, score, or player data. Each viewer fetches the new fragment over HTTP with its existing moderator credentials or signed player cookie. The same-origin socket works on plain HTTP, and its in-process adapter needs no external service. Keep Puma in single-process mode for push delivery.
 - Clients still poll for counters, presence, and recovery. Unchanged phone states return `204`, and counter-only updates don't replace the stage markup or QR image. Clients back off on errors and catch up after reconnecting, including after the quiz ends. Unconfirmed actions are not blindly retried.
 - A player remains present for 20 seconds after their last check-in. Polling renews that lease at most once every five seconds. The open counter compares answers from present players with the number present; finished totals retain all accepted answers, even if someone has gone offline. Player HTML (including live updates) is built only from the current phase, and does not contain correctness flags or later rounds during an open question.
 
+## Round timer
+
+- Timer minutes and seconds are local quiz settings, not part of quiz JSON. A game snapshots their combined duration at launch; zero leaves the game untimed.
+- Opening a timed round persists an absolute deadline. Only moderator stage markup includes the remaining time. The browser ticks against its monotonic clock, but the server owns the deadline and rejects answers received at or after it.
+- A small Puma background task sweeps indexed deadlines four times per second and on boot, so expiration works without viewers and recovers after a restart. It rechecks the current round under the same lock as **Finish** and calls the same closing transition. Both paths broadcast the committed revision; neither can close a newer round whose deadline has not arrived.
+
 ## Scores and prizes
 
 - The server records one opening timestamp per round and stores the elapsed microseconds for a player's last accepted pick, including a repeated pick of the same option. Timing is measured on the server, not from a phone clock. It starts at the shared opening time, not at each player's page load.
